const reportData = [{"title":"Introduction","text":"INTRODUCTION In recent years, convenience stores are no longer simply places for quick purchases – they have quietly become an integral part of modern urban life. For young people and office workers, they are a morning stop for a cup of coffee, a small workspace between meetings, or simply a place to relax after a long day. The clever combination of shopping, dining, and the overall experience has transformed this model into a familiar touchpoint in the lives of millions of Vietnamese people. In that painting, Circle K and GS25 Two brands have emerged as prime examples – competing not only in product categories and network coverage, but also differing profoundly in how each constructs its own \"world.\" One embodies a familiar, approachable, and mainstream style; the other evokes a fresh, youthful feeling with a distinct Korean cultural influence. Each brand tells a different story about how to reach and retain customers. This intriguing contrast raises thought-provoking questions: How do customers truly perceive and feel about these two brands? What draws them in – and what brings them back? How have marketing strategies shaped consumer experience and loyalty? From the questions there This study was conducted to analyze... customer brand awareness This study examines Circle K and GS25 through specific lenses: store space, product catalog, service quality, and communication activities. Based on this, the research aims to evaluate the effectiveness of their current brand positioning and propose strategic directions to help both brands strengthen their competitive position in an increasingly dynamic market."},{"title":"Lời Cảm Ơn","text":"ADMIT Completing this project was a journey that not only required serious research but also encompassed a wide range of emotions – from excitement and curiosity to moments of difficulty, challenges, and even uncertainty. It was a time when we not only gained knowledge but also learned teamwork, how to cope with pressure, and, most importantly, how to continuously improve ourselves. We are well aware that, to complete this project, in addition to individual effort, we also received invaluable support from many sources. First of all, we would like to express our deepest gratitude to the teacher. Bui Nguyen Ngoc Duong – He wholeheartedly guided, advised, and directed us in completing our project. His guidance not only helped us consolidate our knowledge but also broadened our thinking and improved our research skills. To carry out the project \" A STUDY ON CUSTOMER BRAND PERCEPTION OF CIRCLE K AND GS25 BRANDS \"Our group proactively learned, researched, and directly experienced the services at both Circle K and GS25. At the same time, we also applied the knowledge we had learned through discussions with instructors and others around us.\" Furthermore, we must also mention the team members and friends who have always been there to support and encourage each other throughout the project. It was their collaboration and shared effort that helped us complete this research in the best possible way. Finally, we would like to thank the staff of both Circle K and GS25, as well as all the survey participants and customers who took the time to share their opinions. These contributions have helped us collect a wealth of practical data. Despite our best efforts, this research paper inevitably contains some shortcomings. We sincerely hope to receive constructive feedback so that we can continue to improve and develop it further in the future."},{"title":"Chapter 1: Overview of the Topic","text":"CHAPTER 1: OVERVIEW OF THE TOPIC 1. Market context and research problem Over the past decade, the Vietnamese convenience store market has transformed from a nascent segment into one of the most vibrant growth areas of the retail industry. According to the Ministry of Industry and Trade, the number of convenience stores has surged from less than 1,000 outlets in 2010 to over 3,500 in 2023. Simultaneously, Statesman Modern retail sales are showing sustained growth of 10–15% per year, with convenience stores being the fastest-growing channel. The influx of domestic and international brands has transformed this market into a competitive arena, not only in terms of scale but also in brand positioning and experience. As the market grows, consumers increasingly choose based on emotion—and that's when brands transform from mere identification tools into strategic assets, directly impacting customer choice and loyalty. A Q&Me survey shows that over 60% of young Vietnamese consumers choose convenience stores based on brand familiarity and in-store experience, not simply price. Even more noteworthy, the 18-35 age group accounts for a large proportion of weekly store visits—a clear indication that brand awareness plays a crucial role in shaping repeat consumer behavior. This is why analyzing brand awareness is an indispensable step for any business wanting to maintain a sustainable competitive advantage. Circle K is one of the earliest international brands to enter Vietnam — since 2008 — and has built an undeniable advantage in terms of market coverage. As of this month... 05/2025 This chain has officially opened its second store. 500 , becoming the first international convenience store chain in Vietnam to achieve this milestone. With revenue in 2022 reaching nearly 4,000 billion VND Circle K is currently one of the few foreign chains reporting profits, solidifying its absolute leading position in brand recognition. Its 24/7 model and strategic locations in densely populated residential areas allow the brand to meet immediate consumer needs—especially during late-night hours when many competitors don't serve. However, as consumer expectations shift from convenience to experience, the question arises: will its widespread presence still be enough to retain customers in the long term? Meanwhile, GS25 — which entered the market in 2018 — chose a completely different path. As of now end of 2025 This South Korean brand has expanded its network to more than , 450 stores nationwide. Notably, the rapid \"Northern Expansion\" strategy has reached significant milestones. 50 stores in Hanoi Just nine months after entering this market, GS25 isn't simply competing on quantity, but rather positioning itself based on experience and cultural identity: over 50% of young Vietnamese people admit to being influenced by Korean culture in their food choices. GS25 deliberately capitalizes on this trend through distinctive K-food products and a modern space with ample seating – transforming the point of purchase into a destination. However, whether this cultural appeal is sustainable enough to compete long-term with brands deeply ingrained in the consumption habits of millions remains an open question. The contrast in the strategies of the two brands reflects two typical business philosophies of the modern convenience retail industry: one builds its advantage from presence and convenience, while the other bets on experience and unique identity. However, to date, there has been a lack of empirical studies directly comparing Vietnamese consumers' brand perceptions between these two positioning models. This is the gap that the current research aims to fill—through an analysis of customer brand perceptions of Circle K and GS25, to clarify the core factors driving choice behavior and provide a practical basis for sustainable brand building strategies. 2. Purpose of choosing the topic This study on customer brand awareness of Circle K and GS25 aims not only to analyze the differences between these two prominent convenience store chains in the market, but also to clarify why customers choose, remember, and form impressions of each brand. The core purpose of this study is to identify factors influencing brand awareness, thereby better understanding consumer behavior and the extent to which brands impact purchasing decisions. Through this, the research contributes to providing practical insights into how businesses build image, position their brands, and improve operational efficiency in an increasingly competitive environment. Specifically, the research aims at the following objectives: 2.1 Evaluating and comparing product and service experiences The customer experience at convenience stores directly influences brand perception. This study was conducted to assess customer satisfaction with factors such as product quality, product variety, service, store space, and convenience at Circle K and GS25. From this, it clarifies whether customers perceive differences between the two brands and which factor most strongly influences their choice. 2.2 Analysis of Marketing and Branding Strategies This study aims to understand how two brands approach and build their image in the minds of customers through marketing activities. Specifically, it analyzes how Circle K focuses on convenience and coverage, while GS25 leverages new experiences and cultural elements. The goal is to evaluate the effectiveness of communication and promotional strategies and their impact on brand awareness. 2.3 Assessing Competitive Value in Customer Perception This study aims to determine how customers value each brand, including factors such as price, experience, convenience, and brand image. Through this, the research clarifies Circle K's competitive advantages over GS25 (and vice versa), and identifies which factors play a decisive role in customer choice behavior. 2.4 Provide strategic recommendations and solutions The ultimate goal of this research is to propose solutions to improve brand awareness and enhance business performance for Circle K and GS25. Based on the analysis results, the study aims to provide specific recommendations such as improving customer experience, adjusting marketing strategies, optimizing brand image, and increasing customer engagement. Through this research, the aim is not only to serve academic analysis but also to apply it practically, helping businesses better understand how to build brands, influence customer perceptions, and maintain a competitive advantage in the retail market. 3. Research Methodology To ensure comprehensiveness, objectivity, and scientific reliability, the study employs a combination of complementary methods, ranging from secondary document analysis to primary data collection through surveys and in-depth interviews. Each method plays a distinct role in building a complete picture of consumer brand awareness of Circle K and GS25—two highly competitive convenience store chains in the Vietnamese retail market. The specific methods employed are as follows: Secondary literature review: Before conducting field data collection, the research established a solid theoretical foundation by systematizing and analyzing valuable secondary sources. Specifically, the research team collected and synthesized information from domestic and international retail market reports, academic works on consumer behavior and brand psychology, as well as professional literature on marketing and brand positioning in the modern retail sector. Simultaneously, data related to business strategy, development direction, marketing communication activities, and public customer feedback were selectively extracted from mainstream media channels, brand information pages, and social media platforms of Circle K and GS25. This process not only helped the research team understand the industry context and the development history of each brand but also established the necessary theoretical framework for interpreting and evaluating primary data in a more in-depth and reliable manner. Observations from practice: In addition to reviewing documents, the research team conducted systematic observations at several representative Circle K and GS25 stores at various locations and times of day. This is an essential method for capturing vivid and authentic details that are difficult to obtain through written documents. During the observations, the team focused on recording and evaluating specific factors such as: store layout and design, product variety and display, staff service processes and speed, customer service attitudes and communication skills, customer traffic flow at different times of the day, and the behavior and reactions of shoppers in the physical store. In summary, these observations provide valuable qualitative data, accurately reflecting the point-of-sale experience and clarifying how each brand fulfills its commitments in each direct interaction with consumers. Survey using questionnaires The online survey was designed to be structured and delivered to the right target audience—customers who have had or are currently having real-life experiences at Circle K and GS25. This was a deliberate methodological choice: not everyone was eligible to participate, as data is only valuable when it comes from people who have actually interacted with both brands, not from indirect perceptions or imagined scenarios. The questionnaire's content is structured around four groups of variables, each exploring a different layer of the customer-brand relationship. The first group—shopping habits and frequency—establishes the behavioral foundation: how many times customers visit, when, and under what circumstances. The second group measures brand awareness across multiple layers: not just \"whether they know it\" but \"how much they know it\"—logo, colors, slogan, overall feeling. The third group gathers multi-dimensional assessments of satisfaction levels, ranging from product quality and price to service and shopping environment. The final group—and perhaps most strategically important—aims to identify the factors that truly influence brand choice decisions in specific situations: not general reasons, but reasons within the real-world context. Online surveys are chosen not only for their speed and scalability, but also for a less-discussed reason: anonymity. Without the social pressure of interviewers, participants tend to respond more honestly—especially to questions involving negative reviews or brand comparisons. This is crucial in minimizing response bias, thereby enhancing the reliability of the data collected. In-depth live interview If the questionnaire allows for measurement how much For people who think in a certain way, an in-depth interview will answer the questions. Why That's what they think—and these two questions are not interchangeable. A percentage on a chart might describe behavior, but it can't recreate the moment a customer walks into a store and decides in seconds what to buy, or explain why they remember one brand over another. The interviewees were selected from a group of representative customers with real-world experience at both chains — similar criteria to quantitative surveys, but with a different purpose: not to measure, but to listen. The questionnaire was designed to be consistent for both Circle K and GS25, ensuring that all comparisons are based on the same ground, not distorted by different framing of the issue between the two brands. The interviews were guided through six hierarchical content groups, progressing from the sensory surface to the motivational depth. The first layer explores sensory impressions upon entering the store—the layout, lighting, scent, music: nonverbal cues that the brain processes before any conscious thought is formed. The second layer delves into impulsive shopping behavior—items purchased without prior planning—as this is often where the true appeal of the in-store experience is most clearly revealed."},{"title":"Chapter 2: Overview of Circle K & GS25","text":"CHAPTER 2: OVERVIEW OF THE TWO BRANDS CIRCLE K AND GS25 1. Overview of convenience stores Convenience stores (CVS) first appeared in Vietnam around 2006 with pioneering brands like Shop&Go; however, in the early stages, they did not create much appeal due to the dominance of traditional consumer habits. It wasn't until after 2015, when international chains such as 7-Eleven, Circle K, GS25, and FamilyMart officially entered the market, combined with the rapid expansion of domestic chains like WinMart+ This model has truly exploded and reshaped the shopping behavior of urban consumers. Currently, the convenience store market in Vietnam maintains a high growth rate, estimated at around 12–15% per year in recent times. The total number of convenience stores nationwide has exceeded 6,000, mainly concentrated in major cities such as Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City. Revenue from the modern retail segment (including CVS) accounts for approximately 25–30% of the total retail market, and this figure continues to increase as Vietnam's urbanization rate reaches over 35% and is expected to grow significantly in the coming years. This indicates that CVS still has considerable room for growth. The main target customers of convenience stores are young people aged 16-35, including students and office workers. This group has a high shopping frequency, averaging 3-5 times a week, or even daily. Their spending per purchase ranges from approximately 30,000 to 80,000 VND. Although product prices are higher than in traditional markets, they are willing to pay for the convenience, clean environment, air conditioning, and quick shopping experience. The market is currently witnessing intense competition among major chains. WinMart+ Leading in terms of coverage with thousands of stores scattered throughout residential areas, Circle K and GS25 focus on customer experience with modern store models, on-site dining areas, and 24/7 operation. Some chains like Ministop and FamilyMart differentiate themselves with fast food products and convenient combo meals. The role of convenience stores has expanded significantly, going beyond simply selling fast-moving consumer goods. CVS is becoming a multi-functional \"service station\" in urban life, offering various conveniences such as bill payment, mobile phone top-up, e-commerce order pickup, and even a space for studying, working, or meeting. According to some surveys, about 60-70% of young customers consider convenience stores a regular stop throughout the day, not only for shopping but also for using the space. In an era where Gen Z and Millennials hold the power to spend, the retail race leaves no room for lagging brands — and the numbers from Vietnam's convenience store market (CVS) tell a very clear story. FMCG sales at CVS are growing at 13% year-on-year, among the highest in the region. NielsenIQ During the 2020–2022 period, the entire segment accelerated with a compound annual growth rate of 18.4%, far surpassing most traditional retail channels (Vietdata). By 2025, the market size is estimated to reach VND 12,000 billion and maintain a growth rate of over 13% per year (DPS Media), while the vision until 2032 still maintains a pace of nearly 9% per year. 6Wresearch An interesting paradox: despite its explosive growth, CVS still accounts for less than 1% of total national retail sales — meaning the biggest slice of the pie is yet to come, especially as the middle class continues to expand and urbanization shows no signs of slowing down. A hungry market and an uncompromising generation of consumers — innovation in products, technology, and experiences is no longer a strategy, it's a ticket to entry. 2. Overview of the Circle K brand 2.1. Overview Circle K was founded in 1951 in the United States and is currently owned by the Alimentation Couche-Tard group. It is one of the world's largest convenience store chains, operating on a modern retail model, offering fast-moving consumer goods combined with various convenient services. Circle K is often recognized by its distinctive \"K\" logo within a red circle, signifying simplicity, memorability, and suitability for the fast-paced urban lifestyle. The brand is associated with the image of brightly lit stores operating 24/7, providing continuous service and meeting customers' quick shopping needs at any time of day. Circle K's initial development focused on expanding its store network and offering essential products such as fast food, beverages, and daily necessities. Later, the brand evolved to integrate more on-site spaces and convenient services to enhance the customer experience. Thanks to its convenient, flexible model that suits modern lifestyles, Circle K quickly expanded to many countries around the world. In Vietnam, the brand entered the market in 2008 and gradually became popular, especially among young people, aiming to expand its reach and become a familiar destination in the daily lives of urban consumers. 2.2. Vision, Mission, and Core Values 2.2.1 Vision Circle K aims to become the most popular convenience store chain in Vietnam, targeting customers' minds in their daily consumption situations. This is demonstrated through a strategy of increasing store coverage, combined with a 24/7 operating model to maintain a continuous presence. When customers develop the habit of \"having it when they need it,\" the brand gradually becomes the default choice. Thus, the vision of being \"preferred\" is essentially linked to building consumer habits and brand recall. 2.2.2 Mission Circle K offers customers a pleasant, friendly, and reliable shopping experience with a wide variety of goods, services, and food items, all served quickly and with a warm welcome. Essentially, this is how businesses minimize \"friction\" in customer consumption behavior. The store space is designed to be open and accessible, creating a comfortable feeling rather than just a place for quick transactions. At the same time, integrating multiple services at the same point of sale saves customers time and effort. When combined with delivery channels like Grab, this mission expands from an in-store experience to a multi-channel experience, aligning with the flexible needs of modern consumers. 2.2.3 Core Values Circle K's commitment to customer service is encapsulated in the four Fs (Fresh, Friendly, Fast, and Full). Fresh Circle K doesn't sell high-end food. They sell convenience—ready-to-eat sandwiches, freshly roasted sausages, freshly brewed coffee. This is the fastest-turnover category, contributing about 30–40% of the entire convenience store industry's revenue, and more importantly, creating a daily shopping habit. The \"freshness\" at Circle K doesn't come from premium ingredients—it comes from the process: standardized, constantly replenished, and consistent across all stores. Friendly There's no need for staff with expert product knowledge. Just be quick, polite, and avoid upsetting customers. With the main target audience being students and young people, a pleasant shopping experience is a competitive advantage. But this is also the most vulnerable point — just a few substandard stores can damage the brand's \"friendly\" image. Fast A transaction at Circle K takes an average of 1–3 minutes. \"Fast\" isn't just about quick payment—it encompasses the entire journey: intuitive product layout, straightforward selection, and a frictionless process. The real goal is to help customers make quick decisions, not just pay quickly. Full – Complete A Circle K store typically stocks around 2,000–3,000 product codes—from food and drinks to essentials and services like top-ups and bill payments. This is the ambitious \"all-in-one\" concept miniaturized. It increases the value of each order, but also demands extremely disciplined catalog management—because even a slight lapse in control can lead to inventory buildup, duplicate product categories, and the so-called \"fullness\" becoming an operational burden. 2.3. History of Formation and Development 2.3.1. In the international market 2.3.1.1 The period of establishing the premise (1951 – 1978) In 1951, businessman Fred Hervey laid the foundation for his empire by acquiring three \"Kay's Food Stores\" in El Paso, Texas, USA, and renaming them Circle K Food Stores, Inc. 1950s - 1970s: The company expanded its network to neighboring states such as New Mexico and Arizona. By 1975, the system had reached 1,000 stores in the US domestic market. 2.3.1.2. Initial steps towards international expansion and leapfrog growth (1979 – 1990) In 1979, Circle K officially entered the international market through a licensing agreement to establish its first store in Japan. Period 1983–1990: Under the leadership of CEO Karl Eller, the company entered a period of explosive growth, becoming the largest publicly owned convenience store chain in the United States. By 1990, Circle K had approximately 1,300 franchised or joint-venture stores in 13 countries. 2.3.1.3. Period of Ownership Change and Transfer (1990 – 2003) 1990: Due to the impact of the economic recession, Circle K was forced to file for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection. Period 1993 – 2003: After overcoming the crisis, ownership of the company continuously changed hands among large corporations such as Investcorp (1993), Tosco Corporation (1996) and Phillips Petroleum (later ConocoPhillips) in 2001. 2.3.1.4. The Couche-Tard Era and Global Brand Strategy (2003 – Present) In 2003: The multinational corporation Alimentation Couche-Tard (Canada) acquired Circle K for $830 million, marking a significant turning point in the brand's history. In 2015, Couche-Tard announced a repositioning strategy, choosing Circle K as its sole global brand to replace older brands such as Mac's (Canada), Statoil (Europe), and Kangaroo Express (USA). In 2020, Circle K acquired the entire network in Hong Kong and continued to strengthen its position in Asia, including Vietnam. As of February 2020, the system owned more than 14,800 stores worldwide. 2.3.2. In the Vietnamese market 2.3.2.1. Penetration Phase (2008 - 2011) December 2008: Circle K officially entered Vietnam through a franchise agreement with Circle K Co., Ltd. The first store opened in Ho Chi Minh City, becoming the first international convenience store chain in Vietnam. 2.3.2.2. Restructuring and Model Shaping Phase (2012 - 2014) After a period of exploration, Circle K implemented a strategic shift: transforming from a purely retail model to the \"4 Fs\" model (Fresh - Friendly - Fast - Full). In particular, the company pioneered the integration of on-site dining areas, air conditioning, and 24/7 Wi-Fi, creating a new consumer trend for young Vietnamese people. 2.3.2.3. Nationwide Expansion Phase (2015 - 2024) 2015: Expanded the market to the North with the first store in Hanoi (June). Period 2017 - 2019: Continuous influx into tourist cities and economic centers such as Ha Long, Vung Tau, and Can Tho. 2020-2024: Surpassing 400 stores, maintaining its leading position in terms of the number of outlets in the convenience store segment (CVS) in Vietnam. 2.3.2.4. Acceleration Phase (2024 - Present) Early 2024: Focus on optimizing existing stores and upgrading the digital payment system. Circle K maintains its position as the convenience store chain with the largest coverage in key cities such as Ho Chi Minh City and Hanoi. May 28, 2025 – A historic milestone: Circle K officially opens its 500th store in Hanoi. This event marks a significant turning point, making Circle K the first foreign chain to reach this milestone in Vietnam. Late 2025: Senior management changes. The company appoints a new CEO with over 35 years of retail experience to prepare for long-term goals. 2026 (Present): Circle K is implementing its \"Towards 1,000 stores\" plan. Currently, Circle K's size is double that of its second-largest competitor, GS25, and equals the combined number of stores of 7-Eleven and FamilyMart. 2.4. The meaning of Circle K's brand, logo, and slogan."},{"title":"Chapter 3: Survey Results","text":"CHAPTER 3: SURVEY RESULTS FOR 2 BRANDS Building upon the research methodology developed in Chapter 1, including the design of the online questionnaire and the conduct of the quantitative survey, Chapter 3 presents all the collected results along with in-depth analysis and interpretation. The content is presented sequentially, from survey sample characteristics, brand recognition, and consumer behavior to service quality assessment, aiming to provide a comprehensive picture of the competitive landscape between Circle K and GS25 in the Vietnamese convenience retail market. 1. Describe the survey participants. The research team conducted an analysis of the target customer profiles of two convenience store chains based on data collected from online questionnaires. In total, the study collected 93 valid survey samples, which relatively clearly reflected the characteristics of the customer groups currently using Circle K and GS25 services in the Vietnamese market. Based on this data, specific figures are presented and analyzed in detail below to clarify customer behavior, needs, and brand awareness of the two brands. 1.1 Regarding gender From the 93 survey samples we compiled: 59% of the population are female. 64,1% 28, male gender accounts for 30,4% The five people who did not want to reveal their identities accounted for... 5,4% The survey results show an uneven distribution of gender within the sample, with females accounting for the largest proportion, followed by males at 30.4%, and those who do not wish to disclose their gender at 5.4%. Before proceeding with the analysis, it is important to recognize that this distribution reflects the characteristics of the sample more than the actual market situation, as the data was collected from 93 online survey participants—a method inherently susceptible to the influence of the access channel and target audience. The dominance of women in the sample raises questions about the representativeness of the data. Specifically, the evaluations of Circle K and GS25 may be biased towards the consumption trends of this group, especially in experiential criteria such as convenience, shopping environment, or fast-moving consumer goods—factors sensitive to gender differences in purchasing behavior. However, this does not necessarily mean that these two brands actually attract more women in reality. To draw a well-founded conclusion, further comparison with market data or direct observation of behavior at the point of sale is needed. Conversely, the 30.4% male demographic indicates that men are not a minority in the consumer structure at convenience stores. This implies that the convenience store business model is not limited or defined by a specific gender. From a strategic perspective, if businesses rely solely on the current demographic profile to develop their marketing plans, they risk overlooking a significant male customer segment—one that could offer equally important consumer value. Finally, despite its modest 5.4% representation, the gender-nonconforming group is a noteworthy signal. Its presence reflects a trend toward diversification in modern consumers' perceptions of personal identity. For brands, this serves as a reminder to maintain a neutral and inclusive approach, avoiding overly gender-biased positioning—as any unintentional exclusion can narrow the potential customer base in the long term. 1.2 Regarding age After conducting a survey, we found that the age range of shoppers at Circle K and GS25 is very diverse, including people from under 14 to over 34 years old. The 18-24 age group has the highest percentage, followed by the 14-18 age group. According to the survey results: No one under 14 years old is present. 14 people aged 14-18, accounting for... 15,1% 63 people aged 18-24, accounting for... 67,7% 11 people aged 25-34, accounting for... 11.8% 5 people are over 34 years old, accounting for... 5,4% This indicates that Circle K and GS25's primary customers are concentrated in this group. 18-24 years old This group consists of students and young working professionals who need quick, convenient shopping and frequently visit convenience stores. 14-18 years old This group ranks second in terms of popularity, reflecting the appeal of the two brands to students and young people. Meanwhile, the other group... 25 - 34 years old and over 34 years old The lower proportion suggests that older consumers frequent convenience stores less often, possibly due to differing shopping habits. Overall, Circle K and GS25 have a young, dynamic customer base, which is a significant advantage for developing marketing strategies targeting young people. 1.3 Occupation According to the survey results: 68 people belonged to the Student group, accounting for 73%, 9% Nine people belong to the Office Staff group, accounting for... 9,8% 7 people belong to the Self-employed group, accounting for... 7,6% 8 people belong to the \"Other\" group, accounting for... 8,7% Through our survey, we found that the customer base of Circle K and GS25 has a certain characteristic. clear differentiation according to occupation , in which students make up the overwhelming majority, with a proportion reaching... 73,9% This figure clearly reflects the current consumption patterns of young Vietnamese people, as convenience stores are gradually becoming an indispensable part of their daily routines — from buying breakfast and drinks during school breaks to essential personal items. Group office staff Coming in second with 9.8%, this also indicates a noteworthy potential customer segment. With busy work schedules, office workers often tend to choose convenience stores to save time, especially during peak hours such as early morning, lunch break, or after work. Circle K and GS25, with their operating models, are prime examples. 24/7 This is the right choice for this target group. Group freelance business This group accounts for 7.6%, and although the percentage isn't high, they are a group with flexible spending habits and frequent travel, making it easy for them to visit convenience stores at any time of day. \"Other\" This figure, at 0.7%, may include housewives, retirees, or people in other specific professions, indicating that Circle K and GS25 are not limited to a single customer group but have a relatively broad appeal. Overall, the survey results show that Circle K and GS25 are clear positioning This segment targets young, dynamic, and busy customers. This forms a crucial foundation for both brands to continue developing marketing strategies aimed at students—such as school-related combo promotions, membership card point accumulation, or expanding branches near universities and student-dense residential areas—in order to consolidate and expand market share in the future. 1.4 Income Levels According to the survey results: 31 people belong to the group with no income, accounting for... 33,3% 29 people belong to the group with incomes under 5 million VND, accounting for... 31,2% 22 people belonged to the group with incomes ranging from 5 to 12 million VND, accounting for... 24,7% Seven people belonged to the group with incomes ranging from 12 to 20 million VND, accounting for... 7,6% 3 people belonged to the group with over 20 million VND, accounting for... 3,3% Through the survey, we found that the income structure of the participants varied. clear differentiation , in which the group without income accounts for the highest proportion with 33,7% This figure is entirely consistent with the survey results on occupations, where students make up the majority, primarily dependent on family finances and not yet financially independent. The shopping behavior of this group is often characterized by emotions and impulses Rather than careful consideration, they are easily attracted by promotions, free gifts, or eye-catching store spaces. Group under 5 million VND Coming in second with 31.5%, this could include students working part-time, unskilled laborers, or those just starting their careers. Combining these two groups, the total reaches... 65,2% Survey participants had very low or no income — this is a significant number, clearly reflecting the core customer base of Circle K and GS25, who are people with very low or no income — this is a noteworthy figure, clearly reflecting the fact that Circle K and GS25's main customer base consists of these people. Prioritize convenience while maintaining reasonable spending. . Group 5 - 12 million VND This group accounts for 23.9%, typically consisting of part-time students or recent graduates, who have more flexible spending habits and are willing to try new products at convenience stores. Meanwhile, the other group... 12 - 20 million VND and over 20 million VND These figures represent very small percentages, at 7.6% and 3.3% respectively, indicating that the high-income customer segment is not the primary target audience for these two brands. Overall, the survey results show that Circle K and GS25 are primarily serving the following demographics: low-to-middle income segment This is an important foundation for the two brands to continue orienting their product development and pricing strategies, focusing on essential, affordable, and accessible items, while also boosting promotions, loyalty programs, and attractive offers to retain young customers—who are price-sensitive but visit convenience stores very frequently. 2. Consumer behavior and product experience 2.1 Location Based on survey results regarding shopping destination choices, the data reveals a clear shift in modern consumer patterns. Specifically: Convenience Store: Leading the list with 65,2% (60/92 respondents chose this option). This figure confirms the dominant position of the 24/7 retail model in urban lifestyles. With the survey's focus on young people, the fact that Circle K or GS25 are the top priority shows that today's consumers value \"time\" and \"availability\" above all else. They are willing to pay for products at these stores in exchange for convenient location and quick service. Online shopping (E-commerce): Ranked second with 58,7% (54/92 people). Instead of depending on external factors, this growth stems from the widespread use of smartphones and e-commerce platforms (Shopee, TikTok Shop, Lazada). Online shopping reflects a smart consumer mindset: prioritizing price comparison, saving travel time, and taking advantage of digital promotions. Supermarkets and Traditional Markets: It continues to maintain a stable appeal with the following percentages: 52,2% and 40,2% This demonstrates that, despite the booming development of convenient retail channels, consumers still maintain the habit of shopping in person for product categories requiring verification of freshness or when needing to purchase large quantities for their families. Traditional markets persist thanks to their price advantages and community connections within residential areas. Shopping malls (33.7%) and Specialty stores (6.5%) These channels account for a significantly lower percentage. This result is entirely consistent with market reality: these channels are typically only used by customers when high-end needs arise, for shopping-tainment purposes, or for searching for specialized items, rather than for essential daily needs. Overall assessment: The data reflects a trend. Omni-channel consumption This is evident. Consumers are no longer confined to a single form of shopping but are flexible and adapt to changing circumstances. However, the \"convenience\" factor (including the proximity of physical stores and the ease of use of online stores) has become the guiding principle shaping customer shopping behavior in this new era. This presents a golden opportunity for brands like Circle K and GS25 to further expand their reach and optimize the in-store experience. 2.2 Frequency According to the survey results: Group of buyers every 3 days accounts for the highest percentage, approximately 35 - 40% Group seldom"},{"title":"Chapter 4: Comments & Evaluation","text":"CHAPTER 4: COMMENTS AND EVALUATIONS OF THE TWO BRANDS CIRCLE K AND GS25 1. Overview of the two brands 1.1. Circle K brand Circle K is a global convenience store chain, part of Alimentation Couche-Tard, founded in El Paso. Having entered Vietnam in 2008, this brand is not simply an early adopter — they have shaped how Vietnamese people understand modern convenience stores for over a decade. Circle K's greatest strength lies in its extensive coverage and consistent presence. With over 450 stores concentrated in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City, operating 24/7 and strategically located near schools, residential areas, and major transportation routes, Circle K virtually has no blind spots in urban coverage. The interior spaces incorporate seating areas, Wi-Fi, and air conditioning, making the stores familiar stops in daily life. This is why the name \"Circle K\" has long been associated with the consumption habits of generations of students and working professionals in major cities. Furthermore, Circle K boasts one of the highest brand recognition levels in Vietnam's industry. Its distinctive red signage, consistent operating model, and diverse product ecosystem—from FMCG and food services like sandwiches, instant noodles, and iced coffee to convenience services such as bill payments and mobile phone top-ups—have made Circle K a true \"multi-purpose service point\" in urban life. The strategy of localizing its products, rather than adopting the same international catalog, is also a key factor in maintaining its appeal over the years. Financially, Circle K currently holds approximately 38% of the convenience store revenue market share as of 2023—a significant lead over its competitors—thanks to its exclusive franchise model through Circle K Co., Ltd., which allows for rapid expansion without inflating operating costs. However, Circle K also has increasingly noticeable drawbacks. During peak hours, especially in the evenings, the seating area is often overcrowded, directly impacting the experience of even its most loyal customers. The Food Service category, while familiar, is becoming monotonous and lacking innovation — while competitors like GS25 continuously launch new products linked to K-culture trends to create differentiation and encourage exploration. Furthermore, consumer trends are clearly shifting towards healthier products and higher-quality retail experiences. If Circle K doesn't upgrade its product portfolio, improve its retail spaces, and optimize point-of-sale operations, the brand will gradually lose its appeal—not because it's being directly defeated, but because it's being left behind by the changing market itself. With its number one position, Circle K's biggest risk right now doesn't come from a specific competitor, but from complacency with what it has already built. 1.2. GS25 Brand GS25 is a convenience store chain from South Korea, a subsidiary of GS Retail, established in Seoul. Entering Vietnam in 2018, the brand didn't follow the beaten path of its predecessors. Instead, they chose to redefine the convenience store concept through Korean culinary and cultural experiences, creating a different approach within the same market. GS25's greatest strength lies in its ability to generate demand through its products. With over 200 stores primarily concentrated in Ho Chi Minh City and expanding into Hanoi, GS25 doesn't pursue absolute market coverage but focuses on the effectiveness of each individual store. The Food Service category plays a central role, with products like kimbap, tokbokki, bibimbap (mixed rice), fried chicken, and mixed noodles constantly updated to reflect K-culture trends. The store spaces are designed to be spacious, bright, and have clearly defined seating areas, emphasizing an in-store experience. This is why GS25 has quickly become a popular hangout spot for urban youth, especially students. Furthermore, GS25's brand recognition has rapidly increased thanks to a consistent strategy encompassing both products and visuals. Its distinctive green color scheme, modern display, and trend-driven menu create a fresh and engaging experience with every visit. The product ecosystem focuses on ready-to-eat meals and high-value combos, typically priced between 29,000 and 49,000 VND, contributing to higher average order values. Simultaneously, localizing flavors and adapting dishes to suit Vietnamese tastes helps GS25 maintain repeat customer numbers. Financially, while not yet a market leader, GS25 has recorded high growth rates within the convenience store sector due to its focus on customer experience and product offerings. However, GS25 also has some clear limitations. Its limited coverage restricts customer reach, especially when compared to Circle K. During peak hours, the processing and serving areas are easily overloaded due to heavy reliance on Food Service, affecting service speed. Furthermore, product prices are often higher than average, putting pressure on budget-conscious customers. Furthermore, consumer trends are shifting towards a balance between convenience, health, and usability. If GS25 doesn't expand its FMCG portfolio, add healthy products, and optimize in-store operations, it will struggle to reach customers beyond the Gen Z group who love K-culture. GS25's biggest risk isn't direct competition, but being limited by a narrow positioning. To go further, the brand needs to transform from a trendy destination into a comprehensive system of convenience in daily life. 2. Customer care policies of Circle K and GS25 2.1. Circle K brand Currently, Circle K has built a clearer customer care system compared to many other convenience retail chains in Vietnam. The brand doesn't focus on complex after-sales service. They prioritize a fast, convenient experience and maintain high purchase frequency through in-store activities and digital platforms. Loyalty program: Circle K has implemented the CK Club membership system. Customers register for an account to earn points when shopping. Accumulated points can be redeemed for discounts, gifts, or participation in exclusive promotional programs. This system helps increase repeat business and create daily shopping habits. Promotion and discount policies: Circle K maintains discount programs, combo deals, and timed promotions. These programs focus on fast food and beverages. Customers have access to ongoing offers through stores, social media, and the mobile app. In-store services: Circle K operates its stores 24/7. The space is equipped with tables, chairs, Wi-Fi, and air conditioning. Customers can use it as a stopover, for studying, or for short-term work. This enhances the customer experience and encourages them to stay longer in the store. Customer support and feedback policy: Circle K provides support channels such as a hotline, fanpage, and in-store staff. Complaints are received and processed directly. The processing procedure focuses on speed and convenience. Protecting and using customer data: Circle K collects data through its app and purchase transactions. This data is used to analyze consumer behavior, thereby adjusting promotions and product offerings to suit each region. 2.2 GS25 Brand GS25 builds its customer care strategy around four core pillars: Technology, Experience, Exclusive Products, and Convenience. The brand pursues a \"premiumization\" approach in the convenience store segment, prioritizing increasing value per customer rather than racing to expand its network of stores. As of the end of 2023, GS25 operated over 200 stores, primarily concentrated in Ho Chi Minh City, Binh Duong, Dong Nai, and Hanoi. Customer loyalty program and CRM system: GS25 goes beyond the traditional points-based model. The brand leverages shopping behavior data to personalize offers for each customer through the GS25 VN app – a platform managing millions of user profiles. Each purchase earns customers 1% reward points, and the app regularly pushes personalized discount coupons – such as a 50% discount on food items when purchasing drinks – based on their transaction history. The effectiveness of this model is evident in the fact that the rate of returning customers via the app is approximately 20-30% higher than that of walk-in customers. Korean product and food strategy: GS25 focuses on the in-store dining experience. Fresh food and ready-to-eat meals account for a significant portion of revenue, including private-brand products. YOUUS GS Retail's exclusivity contributes significantly to its profit margins. The EAT-IN counters offer tokbokki, fried chicken, and rice balls made with standardized Korean recipes, ensuring consistent quality across its more than 200 stores—a direct commitment to the customer experience. Store space and service quality GS25 invests heavily in its store space, with each store typically ranging from 100 to 150 square meters. The space is equipped with seating areas, power outlets, free Wi-Fi, and 24/7 air conditioning, making it a popular stop for students and freelancers. In terms of service, staff adhere to the \"greet within 3 seconds\" rule and maintain a checkout time of under 60 seconds, especially during peak hours from 11 am to 1 pm. Diversify utility services GS25 operates on an \"all-in-one station\" model, where the store is not just a place to shop but also addresses many daily needs. The brand collaborates with Payoo and MoMo to support payment of electricity, water, internet bills and installment loans. Delivery services are integrated into the platform. ShopeeFood , GrabMart And Beamin, along with policies like free shipping or gifts included with orders during peak periods, helps maintain customer satisfaction. Additionally, many stores place ATMs inside or in front of their premises, increasing convenience and safety for customers. Support and feedback handling policy: GS25 maintains a hotline at 1900 63 60 78 and an official fanpage with an average response time of 15 to 30 minutes during business hours. In case of food quality issues, the store handles them immediately: customers receive a replacement product or a refund along with a direct apology from the manager. The process is designed to be simple, prioritizing speed and customer satisfaction. Exploiting and protecting customer data: Data collected through the app and in-store transactions is analyzed by GS25 to understand consumer behavior across different customer groups and geographic areas. This allows the brand to flexibly adjust its product portfolio, design suitable promotional programs, and implement personalized campaigns with clear objectives—all aimed at improving customer retention and increasing the value of each order. 3. Factors that make customers return 3.1 Circle K 3.1.1 Maximum Convenience Open 24/7: This is Circle K's most powerful and difficult-to-replicate competitive \"weapon.\" In a city where the pace of life increasingly extends into the late night, knowing that there's always a place open—whether it's 2 AM, a holiday, or the first day of the Lunar New Year—creates an irreplaceable sense of security. It's not just convenience, it's a commitment to customers. Prime location: Circle K doesn't place stores randomly — each location is carefully selected, ideally on a street corner, near schools, office buildings, or densely populated residential areas. As a result, customers can almost always stop by without significantly deviating from their usual route, making shopping a natural reflex rather than a deliberate decision. Integrated services: In addition to shopping, Circle K also allows customers to pay utility bills, top up mobile phone credit, withdraw money from ATMs, and receive deliveries on behalf of others right at the counter. This integration transforms Circle K from a typical convenience store into a \"miniature service center,\" helping customers handle multiple tasks in a single visit. Comfortable seating: Air conditioning, power outlets, and free Wi-Fi are seemingly small things, b"},{"title":"Chapter 5: Brand Development","text":"CHAPTER 5: CONTRIBUTING TO BRAND DEVELOPMENT The analyses in the previous chapters have outlined a relatively clear picture of how the two brands, Circle K and GS25, are operating, competing, and positioning themselves in the minds of Vietnamese consumers. If the previous chapters answered the question... What is the current situation? This chapter then raises a more strategic question: What needs to be done, and why is that so important? To ensure a systematic approach, the proposals in this chapter are not presented as a list of isolated solutions, but are guided by a consistent theoretical framework. Specifically, three theoretical foundations are used throughout: Keller's Customer-Based Brand Equity model, Brand Touchpoint theory, and Brand Engagement theory. These three pillars are not separate but complement each other: perception creates the foundation, touchpoints transform perception into feeling, and engagement is the ultimate result of the entire process. From that perspective, the suggestions below are not aimed at simply improving operations or marketing, but at something more fundamental: building consistency between what the brand does. promise and what customers actually experience . 1. Recommendations for Circle K's brand development strategy. With its extensive network and established brand recognition over the years, Circle K holds a relatively strong position in the mainstream convenience store segment. However, position doesn't equate to sustainable advantage. Survey data reflects a worrying reality: the in-store experience is inconsistent across stores, and this inconsistency is quietly eroding customer satisfaction and repurchase intentions. According to Keller (2001), sustainable brand value does not come from mere recognition but from consistent perceived quality —that is, the brand's ability to recreate the same positive feeling at every touchpoint, every moment. This is the core weakness that Circle K needs to prioritize addressing. 1.1. Enhancing the Point-of-Sale Experience — When Space Becomes the Message The most prominent issue lies not in the strategy or messaging, but in the operations—specifically, the stark disparity in quality between stores. While some locations maintain clean spaces and a well-organized layout, others suffer from mixed food odors or disorganized merchandise. At first glance, this might seem like a simple operational management problem, but from a branding perspective, its consequences are far more far-reaching. The theory of brand touchpoints suggests that each time a customer enters a store, the brand is \"speaking\"—not through words, but through space, scent, lighting, and merchandise arrangement. When these touchpoints conflict between stores, the consumer's brain fails to synthesize a clear brand image. Instead, they form perceptions based on their individual experiences. closest and most negative — a psychological mechanism known as the negativity bias, in which bad experiences carry significantly more psychological weight than good experiences. In the context of quick shopping—stopping late at night for food, pausing for a few minutes along the way—the time spent interacting with a brand is short, but the intensity of the perceived experience is very high. A single unpleasant odor or cluttered aisle is enough to make customers leave immediately and move on to a nearby option. Conversely, a spacious, tidy, and easily accessible environment not only retains customers but also triggers unintended purchases—what in-store behavior studies call \"off-the-shelf\" purchases. impulse buying facilitation . Therefore, Circle K needs to standardize its operational processes synchronously across the entire system. Specifically, it needs to establish detailed hygiene standards for each functional area, especially the food processing area; invest in ventilation and odor control systems; and restructure the store layout to optimize quick purchase behavior—with essential products always located in the most easily accessible locations. This is not an optional priority. Without improvement, Circle K's advantage in coverage will become meaningless when customers can easily switch to another competitor—also nearby, but offering a more pleasant experience. In other words, in the convenience retail market, physical presence It is a necessary condition, but not a sufficient one. What is decisive is... the quality of that presence . 1.2. Product and Localization Strategy — Convenience Must Be Linked to Familiarity Another limitation noted in the survey is that Circle K's product catalog is not organized logically according to the time of day. Although the product range is quite diverse, the lack of clear purchase suggestions forces customers to make their own choices—which contradicts the core value of the convenience store model: saving time and reducing the burden on shoppers. From a theoretical perspective, this is directly related to the concept. brand relevance — the degree of brand relevance in each specific usage context. Aaker (2012) argues that a brand is only truly strong when it naturally appears in the consumer's mind at the moment the need arises, not when it is recalled through advertising. To achieve this, brands need to position their products within clear consumer contexts, rather than simply offering a broad product catalog. Behavioral data shows that the vast majority of purchasing decisions in convenience stores happen very quickly — especially in the early morning before work or during lunch breaks. An office worker with only five minutes for lunch is unable and unwilling to put together their own meal. They need a pre-packaged, convenient, quick, and satisfying solution. If Circle K doesn't provide that solution, customers will look elsewhere — and the next time, they won't automatically think of Circle K as a lunch option anymore. Based on this reality, Circle K needs to develop product packages based on specific time slots, along with pricing policies that encourage bundled purchases. Simultaneously, increasing the range of local products such as bread, sticky rice, and other characteristic dishes not only caters to taste preferences but is also a crucial step in its localization strategy—an element increasingly recognized in studies of consumer behavior in emerging markets, where consumers tend to prioritize products that are close to their culture and daily routines. A crucial condition that cannot be overlooked is consistent quality across the entire system. A local product that is well-made in one store but poor-quality in another is not just a product failure—it shatters trust in the brand, which is built on expectations of consistency. This also represents a clear strategic divergence between the two brands: while GS25 leverages novelty and culture as its differentiating factors, Circle K needs to go in the opposite direction—strengthening its advantage in convenience, familiarity, and the ability to reliably and quickly meet daily needs. Without clearly positioning itself in this direction, the brand could easily fall into a murky area in consumer perception—not as innovative as GS25, but also not convenient or familiar enough to become a habit. 1.3 Digital transformation and personalized customer experience — Technology serving people In the context of consumer behavior increasingly intertwined with mobile devices, apps are no longer just supplementary channels but have become independent brand touchpoints—sometimes even more frequent than visits to physical stores. However, current reality shows that Circle K's app has not yet created enough clear value to foster regular usage habits among users. The core reason lies not in the interface or features, but in the depth of personalization. Brand engagement theory emphasizes that true engagement only forms when customers feel a connection to the brand. understand them — not in the sense of general demographic data, but in the sense of recognizing the specific habits, preferences, and lifestyles of each individual. Mass promotional programs, however attractive in terms of material value, still create a psychological distance because they unintentionally convey a message: We don't really know who you are. . Conversely, a coffee offer appearing precisely at 7 a.m. for regular morning coffee buyers—even if of lower value—creates a completely different psychological effect: a feeling of being recognized and cared for. This is the foundation of what marketing researchers call a marketing incentive. perceived relationship quality — Perceived quality in the relationship between a brand and its customers, one of the strongest prerequisites for long-term loyalty. Therefore, Circle K needs to invest in a behavioral data analytics system to segment and personalize offers for specific customer groups. At the same time, it needs to control the frequency of interactions to avoid becoming a source of annoyance—because in digital communication, the line between care about and bother This is very fragile and highly dependent on the relevance of the content. The deployment roadmap should start in key areas before scaling up, both to control quality and to gather field data for refining the model before committing system-wide resources. Without improvement, the app will continue to be an overlooked digital asset — and more importantly, Circle K will lose a channel of contact that can maintain relationships with customers even when they are not near the store. 2. Recommendations for GS25's Brand Development Strategy Despite entering the market later, GS25 possesses an advantage that is not easily replicated: positioning itself with Korean culture — a trend that remains very strong in the minds of young Vietnamese consumers. However, the advantage derived from culture is, in essence, a form of brand association — Its strength depends on the degree of consistency between what is communicated and what is experienced. When these two dimensions conflict, the brand link is not strengthened but, on the contrary, gradually eroded. The research results reveal that this is GS25's current challenge: the gap that remains between the brand image built through media and the actual experience of stepping into a store. If this gap is not narrowed, it will gradually diminish the very thing that created its initial appeal. 2.1 Synchronizing Brand Identity — From Visuals to Emotions GS25 has effectively promoted images associated with Korean culture through its marketing — but a gap still exists between the expectations built through media coverage and the actual experience upon entering the store. At some locations, the distinctive cultural elements are almost entirely absent — the space and operations are no different from a typical convenience store. To understand why this is serious from a branding perspective, we need to look at the mechanism of brand association formation. According to Keller (1993), brand value is built through two core elements: brand awareness (brand recognition) and brand image (Brand image). In this context, brand image is formed from a set of associations stored in the consumer's memory—and these associations only become strong, favorable, and unique when they are repeatedly activated through consistent real-world experiences. If the in-store experience doesn't reflect cultural positioning, that association isn't strengthened but gradually fades—no matter how much media effort is put in. This is important because GS25's customer appeal comes not only from its products, but also from its philosophy. space experience A young person attracted by the Korean Wave entering GS25 will expect to experience that from the very first moment — from the layout and background music to the decorative imagery. If this expectation is not met, not only will the motivation to return significantly decrease, but it will also create the perception that the brand is \"overpromising\" — one of t"},{"title":"Chapter 6: Conclusion","text":"CHAPTER 6: CONCLUSION AND GENERAL IMPRESSIONS OF THE TWO BRANDS Circle K and GS25: The Race for Market Share in the Convenience Store (CVS) Market in Vietnam Looking back at the analysis from the previous chapters, it's clear that the convenience store market (CVS) in Vietnam is no longer simply a battle over the number of outlets or product prices. It's a rapidly maturing market where consumers—especially the younger generation in major cities—have increasingly higher expectations for both product quality and the quality of the experience. In this context, Circle K and GS25 emerge as two representative examples of two completely different strategic approaches, yet both striving towards a common goal: becoming an indispensable part of the daily lives of urban Vietnamese consumers. Circle K, as the first international CVS chain to enter Vietnam in 2008, has built an optimal \"stop shop\" model. The brand's strategy revolves around absolute convenience: a wide selection of essential goods, signature products like Froster shaved ice and instant noodles, and uninterrupted 24/7 service. This is a solid foundation, but it also carries the risk of becoming monotonous if not continuously innovated—especially as the store spaces are gradually becoming outdated compared to younger competitors. GS25, which entered the market in 2018, did not choose a rapid expansion strategy or direct competition in terms of quantity. Instead, with its slogan \"Lifestyle Platform,\" GS25 positions itself as a \"miniature Korea\"—a place where customers not only shop but also experience a lifestyle through exclusive products. YOUUS Fresh food is constantly updated to reflect social media trends, and the space integrates dining, working, and relaxation. This deliberate contrast creates a clear competitive structure: one side optimizes convenience, the other optimizes the experience. 1. The numbers speak for themselves (as of early 2026) Before delving into strategic analysis, it's necessary to look at the actual numbers to understand the true balance of power between the two brands: According to a report by SHS published in November 2025 and data from B&Company, Circle K currently operates approximately 476–500 stores in Vietnam, maintaining its leading position in terms of the number of outlets with dense coverage in Ho Chi Minh City, Hanoi, and many other provinces and cities. Notably, Circle K is one of the few foreign CVS chains in Vietnam to achieve profitability — while most competitors are still accepting losses to gain market share, Circle K reported a profit of over VND 100 billion and maintained its position as the only profitable foreign chain. In terms of market share by revenue, Circle K accounts for approximately 48% of the entire CVS market — a significant lead over its competitors. GS25 is aggressively pursuing its competitors, albeit on a more modest scale. By mid-2024, the chain had approximately 209–230 stores, mostly concentrated in Ho Chi Minh City – where GS25 had surpassed Circle K to lead the market share in the South. In March 2025, GS25 officially expanded northward with the simultaneous opening of its first six branches in Hanoi, ushering in a new phase of competition in the capital city's market – which had previously been almost exclusively Circle K's \"home turf.\" Initial results are remarkable: the average daily revenue of the first six Hanoi stores exceeded 5 million won – 2.5 times higher than the average for convenience stores in South Korea. In terms of revenue, GS25 Vietnam recorded approximately VND 1,690–1,990 billion in 2024, a 29% increase compared to the previous year—an impressive growth rate, even though the company is still accepting losses to invest in expansion. The contribution from the ready-to-eat food segment accounted for over 40% of revenue, clearly reflecting GS25's strategic direction of competing on food quality rather than the number of outlets. In terms of market share by revenue, GS25 currently holds about 14% of the entire CVS market. Looking at these two scenarios, the difference between Circle K and GS25 lies not only in scale but also in their stage of development. Circle K is operating as a defender of its position, focusing on maintaining market share and maximizing profits. GS25 is operating as a challenger, accepting short-term capital expenditures to build a long-term foundation. 2. The Digital Ecosystem Race: From Physical Stores to \"Touchpoints on Phones\" While the period from 2015–2020 saw a race to expand retail locations, from 2022 onwards, the real battle is taking place on consumers' mobile phone screens. By 2026, the competitiveness of CVS chains will no longer be measured solely by prime locations or 24/7 opening hours, but also by their level of integration into the digital ecosystem—where users spend, entertain, and make purchasing decisions every day. Circle K is making significant strides from its traditional loyalty points app to a more multi-functional interactive platform. Linking with popular e-wallets like MoMo and ZaloPay not only simplifies the payment process but also opens up opportunities to access valuable consumer behavior data. Simultaneously, aiming to become a comprehensive \"service ecosystem,\" Circle K is integrating Fintech services, delivery, and bill payment into its system. Gamification campaigns such as \"shake coins,\" \"deal hunting,\" and timed promotions increase user interaction even when they don't have immediate shopping needs. This has important strategic implications: Circle K is trying to maintain a presence in customers' minds even when they're not physically at their counters. This is a step in the right direction given the increasingly high cost of acquiring new customers. GS25 approaches the digitalization challenge from a different perspective, focusing on automation and improving efficiency at the point of sale. Facial recognition payment technology and self-checkout kiosks are gradually replacing part of the traditional service process. More notably, the unmanned store model is being tested in some office buildings—a clear signal of a strategy to reduce operating costs and optimize profitability at each point of sale. If successful, this model could help GS25 scale faster without the corresponding staffing pressure—a significant long-term competitive advantage, especially given that high operating costs are a core weakness of the brand. However, when comparing the two brands overall, the core divergence in their digital strategies remains clear: Circle K is expanding its ecosystem beyond the four walls of its store, striving to become a permanent \"touchpoint\" in consumers' digital lives; while GS25 focuses on deepening the in-store experience—that is, optimizing what happens during the short time customers are in the store. In a market where users' attention spans are increasingly fragmented, the ability to \"retain\" consumers within its own ecosystem will be the deciding factor. 3 Localization Strategies: \"Relaxation\" and \"Lifestyle Aspirations\" One of the most interesting aspects of analyzing the competition between Circle K and GS25 is how the two brands build emotional connections with Vietnamese consumers — and more importantly, how they choose to connect. Localization, for Circle K, is a core strategy and is consistently implemented. The brand understands that Vietnamese consumers—despite rapid urbanization and modernization—still retain deep-seated culinary habits and memories. Therefore, incorporating familiar dishes like mixed noodles, omelets, iced milk coffee, and variations of \"street food\" into convenience store spaces isn't just a tactic to attract customers—it's Circle K positioning itself as a familiar face in everyday life. When a brand makes customers feel so familiar and close, loyalty is built naturally and sustainably, more so than any promotional program. GS25 chose a completely different but equally effective path—at least for its target segment. Instead of blending into the local landscape, GS25 maintained a deliberately \"imported\" identity. The brand positioned itself as a \"gateway to the Korean experience,\" where customers came not just to buy food but also to access a lifestyle. Exclusive GS25-branded products, authentic Korean fresh foods like tteokbokki, gimbap, cold noodles, and imported snacks, along with activities linked to K-Pop culture—all created a system of emotional values ​​that competitors would find difficult to replicate. This is because GS25's appeal doesn't come from individual products, but from a whole cultural story connected to the rapidly spreading Hallyu wave in Vietnam, especially among young Gen Z and Millennials. This cultural appeal explains why GS25's revenue per store is often higher than average — customers don't just drop by, they actively seek them out. From a marketing perspective, these are two different approaches to building brand equity. Circle K builds brand value based on practicality and familiarity—stable elements that are less likely to erode. GS25 builds brand value based on symbolism and emotional experience—elements with the potential to create higher levels of engagement, but also more susceptible to changing cultural trends. In short: Circle K wins on familiarity and practicality; GS25 wins on symbolism and emotional experience. 4. Social responsibility and green consumption trends (ESG) In recent years, ESG (Environmental, Social, and Governance) has gradually shifted from an academic concept to a real factor influencing consumer decisions — especially among young, educated consumers living in major cities. This is precisely the core customer group that both Circle K and GS25 are targeting, making ESG an indispensable competitive variable. Both brands have taken initial steps to demonstrate environmental responsibility. Replacing single-use plastics with more eco-friendly materials, encouraging customers to use reusable bags and straws—these small actions send important signals to increasingly environmentally conscious consumers. Simultaneously, both are gradually expanding their product portfolios toward healthier options—from packaged salads, nut milks, and sugar-free juices to low-calorie food lines—to capitalize on the growing \"healthy lifestyle\" trend among younger consumers. The \"green store\" trend, featuring eco-friendly materials and vegan, low-sugar product lines, is also becoming the next battleground for both brands. However, it must be acknowledged that, at present, the ESG activities of both brands remain more superficial than long-term strategies. Neither brand has truly transformed ESG into a measurable core competitive advantage. Most initiatives are still in the experimental stage, not yet deeply integrated into operational models or consistent brand communication. Current ESG reflects more orientation than differentiation — but in the next few years, as the new generation of consumers matures and becomes more discerning in choosing brands that align with their personal values, this could become a more distinct differentiating factor between the CVS chains. 5. A direct comparison of competitive models. After analyzing each aspect separately, a holistic view is needed to assess the true competitive position of each brand — not only based on what they do well, but also on the weaknesses they face. Circle K possesses two fundamental competitive advantages that rivals will find difficult to match in the short term: network scale and profitability. With nearly 500 outlets nationwide and a dominant revenue market share of 48%, Circle K creates a significant barrier to entry. Furthermore, in an industry where most competitors are operating at a loss, Circle K has demonstrated the sustainability of its business model – a significant strategic advantage. Its ability to quickly meet essential needs and its home delivery services via delivery apps also help the brand maintain its appeal to young, single urban consum"}]
;